From: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] misc: decd: refuse a non-contiguous dma-buf import

dec_dma_map() keeps sg_dma_address(sgt->sgl) and throws the rest of the
scatterlist away. There is no scatter-gather anywhere in this hardware path:
DECD is programmed with one base address and a stride, and the chroma address
is derived from that base arithmetically. So when an import maps to more than
one segment, the hardware reads segment 0 and then keeps walking into whatever
physical memory happens to follow.

That is not theoretical. Submitting Cedrus's own decoded NV12 dma-buf, measured
on the bench:

    PHYS pages=338 first=0x46f7a000 contiguous=NO breaks=31 longest-run=16(64KiB)
    0x05600070 = 0x46F7A000

The base is correct -- it is exactly the buffer's first page -- but only the
first 64 KiB of the frame is really there, which is 51 of 720 luma lines. The
chroma base, computed as y + 0xE1000, lands 924 KiB past the start and so is
unrelated memory entirely, which is why such a frame scans out overwhelmingly
green. The buffer itself is perfect: dumped through the same FD and compared
against a host software decode, it is bit-exact.

The failure is therefore silent and looks exactly like decoder corruption, which
cost this investigation a fence-lifetime fix, a frozen-buffer test and a byte
dump to tell apart. Refuse the import and say why instead, printing the whole
segment layout -- the first segment alone does not distinguish "coalescing was
capped at a limit" from "coalescing never happened", and that is the difference
between a device constraint to fix and an allocator to work around.

Contiguous importers are unaffected: the scanout carveout and any CMA
allocation map as a single segment spanning the whole buffer, which is why
static carveout frames have always displayed correctly.

This is a guard, not the fix, and it earned its place immediately: the first
apparently-successful IOMMU run produced a single IOVA, and the next run of the
same binary on the same boot came back as 14 segments. Without this the former
would have been reported as a fix.

--- a/drivers/misc/sunxi_decd/decd_frame.c
+++ b/drivers/misc/sunxi_decd/decd_frame.c
@@ -672,9 +672,45 @@
 	map->sgt = dma_buf_map_attachment(map->attach, DMA_BIDIRECTIONAL);
 	if (IS_ERR(map->sgt))
 		goto err_detach;
+
+	/*
+	 * There is no scatter-gather anywhere in this hardware path: DECD is
+	 * programmed with one base address and a stride, and the chroma address
+	 * is derived from it arithmetically. Honouring only sgl[0] of a
+	 * multi-segment import therefore does not read a slightly wrong buffer,
+	 * it reads segment 0 and then walks off the end into whatever else is
+	 * resident -- silently, and looking exactly like decoder corruption.
+	 *
+	 * Refuse instead. A contiguous import (the scanout carveout, and CMA)
+	 * is unaffected: it maps as a single segment spanning the whole buffer.
+	 */
+	if (map->sgt->nents != 1 ||
+	    sg_dma_len(map->sgt->sgl) < map->dbuf->size) {
+		struct scatterlist *s;
+		unsigned int i;
+
+		dev_err(dev,
+			"dma-buf import is not contiguous: %u segment(s), first %u of %zu bytes; DECD fetches from a single base and would scan unrelated memory\n",
+			map->sgt->nents, sg_dma_len(map->sgt->sgl),
+			map->dbuf->size);
+		/*
+		 * Print the whole layout. The first segment alone does not say
+		 * whether coalescing was capped at a limit or never happened at
+		 * all, and that distinction is the difference between a device
+		 * constraint to fix and an allocator to work around.
+		 */
+		for_each_sgtable_dma_sg(map->sgt, s, i)
+			dev_err(dev, "  seg %u: %pad + %u\n", i,
+				&s->dma_address, sg_dma_len(s));
+		goto err_unmap;
+	}
+
 	map->dma_addr = sg_dma_address(map->sgt->sgl);
 	return map;
 
+err_unmap:
+	dma_buf_unmap_attachment(map->attach, map->sgt, DMA_BIDIRECTIONAL);
+
 err_detach:
 	dma_buf_detach(map->dbuf, map->attach);
 err_put:
